    <string name="advice_empty_title">The week is still empty</string>
    <string name="advice_empty_body">No time measured since Monday. The review fills in as the days are written.</string>
    <string name="advice_concentrated_title">One app leads</string>
    <string name="advice_concentrated_body">%1$s takes a large share of the week. That is the first place a daily limit changes the total.</string>
    <string name="advice_rising_title">The week is climbing</string>
    <string name="advice_rising_body">Time is clearly above the previous week, on comparable days. A cap on the two longest apps is often enough to bend the curve.</string>
    <string name="advice_falling_title">The week is easing</string>
    <string name="advice_falling_body">Less time than the previous week, on comparable days. Keeping this pace is already a result.</string>
    <string name="advice_late_title">The peak is late</string>
    <string name="advice_late_body">The busiest hour falls late in the evening or during the night. Moving the last stretch earlier lets the night start sooner.</string>
    <string name="advice_unlocks_title">Many unlocks</string>
    <string name="advice_unlocks_body">The phone is picked up very often. Grouping checks into a few visits cuts the back and forth.</string>
    <string name="advice_light_title">A light week</string>
    <string name="advice_light_body">The daily average stays under two hours. Few weeks hold that line without looking at them.</string>
    <string name="advice_weekend_title">The weekend weighs more</string>
    <string name="advice_weekend_body">The busiest day falls on Saturday or Sunday. A slightly lower cap on those days balances the week.</string>
    <string name="advice_steady_title">A settled spread</string>
    <string name="advice_steady_body">No extreme peak, and no sharp gap with the week before. Reading this review each Monday keeps the line.</string>

